---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { SEGMENTS } from '../../data/segments';

const path = '/for';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for');
const desc = 'Choose a Shopify store-type guide by buying cycle, catalog constraints, and customer questions, then compare apps against your actual messaging requirements.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="hero-title text-5xl md:text-6xl">Shopify SMS marketing apps by store type</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-orange-950/80">Start with the buying cycle you need to support. These {SEGMENTS.length} guides help you frame category-specific messaging requirements before choosing an app. A store category is a planning starting point, not evidence that a particular vendor or channel will perform best.</p>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {SEGMENTS.map((segment) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/for/${segment.slug}`}>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-950">{segment.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-orange-950/80">Plan for {segment.pain}.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-orange-950/80">{segment.buyingCycle}.</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Choose the guide closest to the purchase decision</h2>
        <p>If you sell across categories, start with the constraint behind the message: a size-specific restock, a consumable reorder, a gift deadline, or a project that needs more consideration. Read a second guide when its buying cycle also applies. A subscription beauty store, for example, can use both the beauty and subscription-box guides as planning references.</p>
        <p>Write down the customer question, the event that makes the message relevant, and when the message should stop. Use actual inventory, order history, delivery dates, or account status to test the plan. Treat suggested campaigns as hypotheses; the guides do not establish a universal send frequency or promise revenue.</p>
        <h2>Turn the category plan into app requirements</h2>
        <p>For each proposed message, list the data needed, the intended recipient, exclusions, the destination, and the person responsible for replies. Decide whether the customer needs an immediate alert, a detailed explanation, or a service conversation before selecting a channel. Test whether your shortlisted tools can carry out that workflow with your own store data.</p>
        <p>Review consent and sending requirements for the audience and locations you serve before launch. Record expected costs and decide how you will compare a pilot with a comparable group that does not receive the campaign. Evaluate net sales and costs alongside opt-outs and support demand; attributed sales alone do not establish incremental profit.</p>
        <h2>Continue by task</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a class="link" href="/apps">Browse app reviews</a> to build a shortlist.</li>
          <li><a class="link" href="/compare">Open app comparisons</a> once you have two candidates.</li>
          <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases">Explore use-case guides</a> for a specific messaging job.</li>
          <li><a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">Review pricing questions</a> before estimating a pilot budget.</li>
          <li><a class="link" href="/guides/consent">Review consent considerations</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">the TCPA guide</a>, and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> when preparing a sending plan.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
